From: filnix <filnix@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Count a heap page added after a mark as wholly past it

alloc_heap() numbers a new page with the page count before it
(a->num = q->st.hp_num++), so a heap mark (hp_num, hp), as a frame or
a choicepoint keeps one, is offset hp in page hp_num-1. A page numbered
hp_num was therefore added after the mark. trim_heap() and
is_heap_since() read it as the mark's own page instead: the trim kept
its first hp cells, and is_heap_since() called a term in those cells
older than the frame.

Since ccb10b7d ("... let the head rule veto only a tail call") a head
that binds the new frame's variables into such a term no longer stops
the tail call, so refs_trimmed_heap() is the only guard. It let the
reuse through and the trim then cut the term the new frame was still
reading. A clause that builds a list and tail-calls with it fails or
answers wrongly whenever the list starts a new heap page:

    merged([], []).
    merged([R], [R]) :- !.
    merged([A-B, C-D|Rs], Out) :-
        (   C =< B + 1 -> E is max(B, D), merged([A-E|Rs], Out)
        ;   Out = [A-B|Out1], merged([C-D|Rs], Out1)
        ).
    normal(Rs0, Rs) :- msort(Rs0, Rs1), merged(Rs1, Rs).

normal/2 fails for the lists [0-0, 1-1, ..., (N-1)-(N-1)] with N = 9,
59 and 159 when called from a failure-driven loop over N = 1..300.

Treat a page numbered hp_num as added after the mark. trim_heap()
empties it and makes it the current page from its start, which also
puts hp_num back in step with the pages; it keeps the page, as before,
so a loop that backtracks across the boundary does not allocate a page
each pass. is_heap_since() counts the whole page, and
refs_trimmed_heap() no longer returns early when one exists.
---
diff --git a/src/heap.c b/src/heap.c
index b26c7e3b..e889045b 100644
--- a/src/heap.c
+++ b/src/heap.c
@@ -838,6 +838,16 @@ void trim_heap(query *q)
 	if (!q->heap_pages)
 		return;
 
+	// alloc_heap() numbers a page with the count before it, so a mark (hp_num, hp) is an offset into page hp_num-1
+	// and a page numbered hp_num was added after the mark: all of it goes, not just what lies past that offset.
+	// The page stays, so that a loop backtracking over the boundary does not allocate one a pass, and becomes the
+	// current one from its start.
+
+	if (q->heap_pages->num == q->st.hp_num) {
+		q->st.hp = 0;
+		q->st.hp_num++;
+	}
+
 	while (q->heap_pages->idx > q->st.hp) {
 		cell *c = q->heap_pages->cells + --q->heap_pages->idx;
 		unshare_cell(c);
@@ -852,7 +862,9 @@ bool is_heap_since(const query *q, const frame *f, const cell *c)
 {
 	const page *a = q->heap_pages;
 
-	for (; a && (a->num > f->hp_num); a = a->next) {
+	// A page numbered hp_num or more was added after f's mark, see trim_heap().
+
+	for (; a && (a->num >= f->hp_num); a = a->next) {
 		if ((c >= a->cells) && (c < (a->cells + a->idx)))
 			return true;
 	}
diff --git a/src/query.c b/src/query.c
index 2e980582..fc651d0f 100644
--- a/src/query.c
+++ b/src/query.c
@@ -1833,7 +1833,7 @@ static bool refs_trimmed_heap(const query *q, const frame *f, unsigned num_vars)
 {
 	const page *a = q->heap_pages;
 
-	if (!a || ((a->num <= f->hp_num) && (a->idx <= f->hp)))
+	if (!a || ((a->num < f->hp_num) && (a->idx <= f->hp)))
 		return false;
 
 	const frame *f_new = GET_NEW_FRAME();
